"The surah begins with the disjointed letter (huruf al-muqatta'at) \"Nun\". This letter is among the ambiguous (mutashabih) verses in the Quran; that is, its meaning cannot be fully known. The scholars of recitation (qira'ah) mostly recited this letter with izhar (clear pronunciation), while some recited it with idgham (assimilation), either with or without ghunnah (nasalization). The letter Nun, in terms of its script, is in the shape of a dot and a circle. This letter points to the source of speech and writing in human nature (fitrah) and emphasizes its value. The primary purpose here is to demonstrate human helplessness and to test those who are deeply rooted in knowledge.\n\nThe word Nun in Arabic also conveys meanings other than the familiar letter \"nun\". Among these are meanings such as \"fish\" (as in Prophet Jonah [Yunus] being called Dhu al-Nun), \"inkwell\", and the blade of a sword. According to an opinion narrated from Ibn Abbas, it is a great fish upon which the earth rests; this fish is called \"Yahmut\". In another narration, it is related that the first thing Allah created was the pen, then He created the Nun upon the water and spread the earth's crust over it.\n\nThese narrations point to the beginning of creation. First, the \"Supreme Pen\" (al-Qalam al-A'la) was created, and everything that would occur until the Day of Resurrection was written. Then matter was created, after which the heavens were formed from gaseous matter, and the terrestrial globe from its liquid state. This Nun at the center of the earth moves like a fish and causes earthquakes. As for the mountains, they were created to quell this motion, serving as pegs for the earth. These descriptions coincide with certain findings of modern geology.\n\nExegetes (mufassirun) have put forward differing views regarding whether the \"Nun\" here is a letter or a noun. Scholars like al-Zamakhshari stated that it is one of the disjointed letters of the alphabet and cannot be a noun because its ending does not take grammatical inflection (i'rab). Others, relying on narrations from Ibn Abbas, accepted that it means \"fish\" or \"inkwell\". After citing these disagreements, Elmalılı states that the most sound path is to accept this letter as ambiguous (mutashabih) and entrust its ultimate interpretation (ta'wil) to Allah.\n\nThe expression \"Wal-Qalam\" (By the pen) in the surah is also dwelt upon. The pen may be the divine pen that wrote the Preserved Tablet (al-Lawh al-Mahfuz), or it may refer to the genus of pens used by human beings. The first created pen wrote everything that will take place until the Day of Resurrection. The human pen, on the other hand, is the foundation of knowledge and civilization. By teaching humanity to write with the pen, Allah bestowed a great blessing. This oath emphasizes the value of the pen and of writing.\n\nIn summary, the \"Nun\" at the beginning of the surah and the oath taken by it draw attention to the importance of creation and knowledge. This ambiguous letter prompts intellects to deep reflection. The pen, on the other hand, is a symbol of both divine power and human intellect. The surah, by stating that the Prophet (peace be upon him) is upon an exalted character, counsels him to have patience. This introduction serves as a preparation for the main themes of the surah.\n\nThe surah begins with an oath by \"Nun\" and the pen. What is meant by the pen here is the divine pen that writes the truth and what is right. The oath encompasses not only the pen, but also those who write and what is written. What does the writing is not the pen itself, but the beings endowed with intellect who use it; these are the angels or human consciousnesses. This oath is meant to emphasize that the Prophet (peace be upon him) is not mad and that he has been favored with divine blessings.\n\nAs the response to the oath, it is proclaimed to the Prophet (peace be upon him) that, by the grace of his Lord, he is not mad. He has been endowed with sublime qualities such as prophethood and intellect. Furthermore, it is stated that he is upon an unending reward and an exalted character. This exalted character is the etiquette of the Quran and divine morality; Aisha stated that the character of the Prophet was the Quran.\n\nIn the continuation, with the words \"You will see, and they will see,\" it is indicated that it will become clear who is afflicted with madness. It is emphasized that your Lord knows best who has strayed from His path and who is rightly guided. For this reason, the Prophet (peace be upon him) is commanded not to obey the deniers. They wished for the Prophet to be lenient and to compromise, yet he never ceased to speak the truth.\n\nThe Prophet (peace be upon him) is asked not to obey individuals who possess certain vile traits: those who swear many oaths, the contemptible, slanderers, hinderers of good, sinful, coarse, and ignoble (zanim)... These qualities do not target a specific person alone, but generally address such people. They boast of having wealth and sons, yet they are morally base.\n\nWhen the verses are recited to them, these wicked individuals say, \"Fables of the ancients.\" Allah declares that He will brand their snouts (noses) to humiliate them. This is a punishment that will disgrace them in this world or in the Hereafter. Believers must not conform to such people, but should persevere on the path of truth with patience and good character.\n\nAllah had given the deniers in Mecca wealth and children as a trial. This was a test for them. The Prophet (peace be upon him) prayed for the affliction of famine upon the tribe of Mudar; they were punished with a famine that lasted seven years. This verse transitions to the story of the owners of the garden: In Yemen, a righteous man had a vineyard; when he died, his children acted stingily and deprived the poor. They 'swore to harvest the garden in the morning, but did not make an exception (by saying insha'Allah).'\n\nWhile they were asleep, Allah sent a calamity upon the garden, and by morning the garden was reduced to ashes. In the morning, they called out to one another, saying, 'Go early to harvest your produce.' As they went, they whispered secretly to each other, 'Let no poor person enter upon you today.' They acted with tyranny and greed.\n\nWhen they reached the garden and saw it devastated, they said, 'We must have lost our way; rather, we are ruined.' The most moderate among them (awsat, meaning the most prudent and best), whose counsel had previously not been heeded, spoke up: 'Did I not tell you, why did you not glorify Allah? Exalt Allah above all deficiencies, uphold what is right, and make an exception (by saying insha'Allah)!' he said.\n\nThey repented in regret, saying, 'O our Lord, truly we were wrongdoers,' and turned in repentance. They began to reproach one another. They said, 'Perhaps our Lord will give us better than this in its place; indeed, we turn to Him.' Such is the punishment of this world; it awakens. Yet the punishment of the Hereafter is far greater; to be protected from it, one must believe beforehand.\n\nFor the righteous (the people of taqwa), there are Gardens of Delight (Na'im) with their Lord. Would We ever treat Muslims like criminals? Is it written in their book that 'whatever you choose shall be yours'? On the Day of Resurrection, matters will become severe, and they will be summoned to prostrate, but they will not be able to do so. I grant respite to the deniers; indeed, My plan is firm.\n\nThe Prophet is told to be patient and not to be like the companion of the fish (Jonah [Yunus]). He had prayed in anger, but the grace of his Lord reached him. When the disbelievers heard the Quran, they almost knocked you down with their eyes and said, 'He is indeed mad.' Yet the Quran is nothing but a reminder to all the worlds.\n\nIn verses 38–41, the polytheists' claim that \"Whatever we desire shall be ours\" is refuted. Allah has not granted His authority of judgment to anyone. There is neither a book nor a covenant supporting these claims of theirs. If there were such a rule, the tyrants and nations of the past would have remained in this world. Ask them: Which of you can guarantee the truth of this statement? Or do they have partners they associate with Allah? Then let them bring their partners; but they can never alter the truth.\n\nThe word \"saq\" mentioned in verse 42 literally means the shin or the origin of something. Here, it is a metaphor expressing the intensification of the decree of truth on the Day of Resurrection. In a hadith found in al-Bukhari and Muslim, it is narrated that on that day Allah will uncover His \"saq\" and the believers will fall down in prostration. This allegorical (mutashabih) expression has been interpreted as the uncovering of the truth and the mounting of terror. Scholars such as al-Zamakhshari and al-Razi have stated that this word is used to convey the severity and difficulty of calamity. It is not correct to derive any meaning that likens Allah to physical bodies.\n\nIn verse 43, it is related that on the Day of Resurrection the guilty will be called to prostrate, yet they will not be able to do so. While they were safe and sound in this world, they had been invited to prostrate, but they did not accept it. On that day, however, they will desire to prostrate, but will be unable to do so. This is a matter of profound regret and humiliation for them.\n\nIn verses 44–45, it is commanded to leave those who deny the Quran to Allah. They will be led to punishment gradually without realizing it; they will be granted a long respite, and then seized with a firm plan. This shows that what they deem to be good is in fact evil.\n\nIn verses 46–47, it is emphasized that the Prophet does not ask for a reward in return for conveying the message, and that they are not weighed down under debt. Knowledge of the unseen (ghayb) is not in their possession, nor is the pen that writes everything in their hands. Therefore, the judgments they render are false.\n\nIn verses 48–50, the Prophet is advised to be patient and not to be hasty like Jonah (Yunus, peace be upon him). Jonah departed from his people in anger and fell into the belly of the fish, but through Allah's mercy he was saved and became one of the righteous. This story teaches the importance of patience and of being content with Allah's decree.\n\nHere, with reference to the story of the Prophet Jonah (Yunus), it is related that because of a momentary anger he was confined in the belly of the fish, but thanks to the blessing his Lord bestowed upon him, he was delivered and became a righteous servant. This indicates that everything occurs through the writing and decree of Allah, who knows the unseen; there is an order within His determination, and within this order, human beings have also been granted a certain authority.\n\nStates such as anger or patience depend on one end upon Allah's decree and on the other upon human will. Our Prophet (peace be upon him), the possessor of that magnificent character, is commanded not to show impatience like Jonah, but to bear the decree of his Lord with patience and continue his duty. He must look to the judgment of tomorrow and await what the sublime Pen has written in the unseen.\n\nWhen the disbelievers heard the Quran, they would almost strike you down with their eyes. Out of their envy, they were as if ready to cast the evil eye upon you, wishing to destroy you through the malice of their sinister glances. Just as anger has an effect upon the body, the eyes likewise carry a good or bad influence depending on how they gaze.\n\nMuch has been said concerning the evil eye (nazar); some have denied it, while others have accepted it. However, without delving into details, let us state this: the evil eye is real, and, may Allah protect us, being conspicuous is dangerous. Allah makes a shield against the evil eye for those servants whom He wills to protect. The polytheists envied the beauty of the Quran and the Prophet's attaining it; they glared at him as if they would devour him, and then fell into contradiction by claiming, 'He is a madman.'\n\nYet the Quran does not belong to them alone; it is a reminder to all the worlds. Those endowed with pure intellect benefit from it, whereas those who are not bring harm upon themselves. In the end, they depart, groaning in anguish. Surah al-Qalam concludes, just as it began, with 'Nun'. May Allah, the Lord of the worlds, grant success in remembrance and reflection to those who write, read, and listen."